From cd938369f5cb12497f5412f41587287fa8874ef6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 15:50:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix target/reference balancing: drop the hardcoded 1e6 factor

Training collapsed to ~0 loss within a few epochs, on both train and
validation, and the learned density ratio was wrong by three orders of
magnitude.

Stage 2 of ReweightStep computed

    target_scale = reference_train_sum * 1e+6 / target_train_sum

leaving the target class carrying 1e6 times the total weight of the
reference. CARL's optimum is s = w_t.p_t / (w_t.p_t + w_r.p_r), so:

  * r = s/(1-s) estimates factor * p_t/p_r, not the density ratio;
  * the reference contributes ~1e-6 of the loss, so the network minimises it
    by saturating its output towards 1 everywhere. The analytic floor is
    ~1.5e-5, which is the "loss crashes to zero" symptom.

Measured on overlapping 1-D Gaussians, 200 epochs: with the 1e6 the loss goes
0.724 -> 3e-4 and the recovered ratio is 3522x the truth; at 1.0 the loss
settles at 0.6835 and the ratio is right to 0.2%. The README already
documented the formula without the factor, so this restores the documented
and correct behaviour.

- reweighting.py: the factor becomes data.target_balance_factor, default 1.0,
  which balances the classes exactly. Values outside [0.01, 100] raise a
  RuntimeWarning explaining what the network will estimate instead, since the
  failure is otherwise silent and looks like very good training.
- pipeline.py: wire data.target_balance_factor; record it.
- weights_test.py: assert the classes end up on equal total weight, that
  r = s/(1-s) recovers p_t/p_r at factor 1 and factor*p_t/p_r otherwise, and
  that an extreme factor warns.
- weights_test.py: the conditioning check asserted the normalization reduces
  the float32 accumulator, which was only true under the 1e6 regime. With
  balanced classes the raw weights are small, so normalization scales up; the
  invariant is that the accumulator lands at the batch size either way.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01F4Eusp7txs6rm7grkjJvRj
